You do not need any specific degree, title, or academic qualification to submit a paper to CVPR, ICCV, NeurIPS, ICML, or ICLR.
These conferences use a double-blind peer-review process. Reviewers do not see your name, university, or company, so your work is judged purely on its own merit. Undergraduates, high school students, independent researchers, and industry professionals can all submit and publish papers. [1, 2] 
However, to successfully pass peer review, your paper must meet rigorous scientific standards.
## Core Requirements for Acceptance

* Novelty: You must present an original algorithm, a significantly improved architecture, a new theoretical proof, or a groundbreaking application.
* Methodology: Your mathematical derivations must be sound, and your experimental setups must be reproducible.
* Benchmarks: You must test your method against current state-of-the-art models on standard datasets (like ImageNet, COCO, or GLUE) and prove your approach offers an advantage (e.g., higher accuracy, faster speed, or fewer parameters). [3] 
* Clarity: The paper must be written in clear, academic English and formatted using the conference's exact LaTeX template.

## Common Profiles of Submitters
While anyone can submit, the vast majority of accepted papers come from authors with one of the following backgrounds:

* Graduate Students: Master’s and Ph.D. candidates working in AI/ML labs under the guidance of a faculty advisor.
* Industry Researchers: Scientists at corporate labs like Google DeepMind, OpenAI, Meta AI, or Microsoft Research.
* Undergraduate Researchers: High-achieving college students who partner with a university professor or a senior graduate student mentor. [4] 


